  "narrative": "All returned estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1 claims, a positive signed estimate means the exposed group has a more negative dependency score, i.e. stronger dependency; for direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate means weaker dependency. I used the approximate practical 10% score-scale thresholds used previously: MDM2 about 0.0269, USP7/NFE2L2 about 0.0093, SMARCA2 about 0.0415, and WRN about 0.0301.  \n\nThe MTAP-independent MDM2 tests are most informative. H142, MTAP loss among CDKN2A-intact cells, is essentially null: signed effect +0.0042, 95% interval -0.0178 to +0.0263, with the upper bound just below the MDM2 threshold. It does not support an MTAP-associated MDM2-dependency signal independent of CDKN2A loss, so I reject it and close it. H143, MTAP loss in low-copy 9p21 CDKN2A-intact cells, has a point estimate above the MDM2 threshold (+0.0340), but the interval is wide and imprecise (-0.0296 to +0.0976) and the exposed cell is only 25; it cannot distinguish an MTAP signal from sparse noise or deletion-like genomic context. I keep it unresolved and close it, favoring the interpretation that the robust CDKN2A-MDM2 association should not be mechanistically attributed specifically to MTAP from this dataset.  \n\nThe direct research-marker tests do not support new accepted predictor effects. H144, research marker D-positive for MDM2 among CDKN2A-intact/TP53-intact cells, is registered as D-positive stronger dependency, but the signed estimate is negative and the upper bound (+0.0059) is below the MDM2 threshold; the registered stronger-dependency claim is rejected. A possible opposite weaker effect is not accepted because the point estimate is below threshold and the interval still crosses the registered-direction null. H145, research marker E-positive for USP7 among TP53-intact cells, was registered as E-positive weaker USP7 dependency, but the evidence is opposite and statistically significant: signed effect -0.0197, interval -0.0373 to -0.0021, implying E-positive stronger USP7 dependency. The lower magnitude bound is well below the USP7 practical threshold, so although this is a clear opposite signal in this sample, I do not accept a clinically robust reversal; I reject the registered claim and close it. H146, research marker F-positive for NFE2L2 among KEAP1-intact cells, is small (+0.0047) with an interval spanning null and meaningful values; unresolved and closed. H147, marker D-positive for SMARCA2 among SMARCA4-intact cells, is near-null and far below the SMARCA2 threshold (+0.0071; interval -0.0111 to +0.0253), so it is rejected. H148, marker E-positive for WRN among MSI-high-negative cells, is small and opposite (-0.0071; interval -0.0232 to +0.0090), with the entire interval inside the WRN 10% threshold range, so the registered stronger-dependency claim is rejected.  \n\nThe marker/signature interaction tests are mostly inconclusive. H149, whether marker D modifies the CDKN2A-MDM2 effect, is wide and centered on attenuation (-0.0219; interval -0.0912 to +0.0475). It does not support amplification by D-positive status; I close it unresolved. H150, whether marker E modifies the TP53-USP7 effect, is positive but imprecise (+0.0248; interval -0.0146 to +0.0641), so it is unresolved and closed. H151, CDKN2A-MDM2 modified by high research signature A, is the most promising: signed interaction +0.0540 exceeds the MDM2 threshold, but the interval (-0.0396 to +0.1476) is wide and includes null. H152, KEAP1-NFE2L2 modified by high research signature B, is also promising but imprecise (+0.0267; interval -0.0185 to +0.0718). I keep these two unresolved and active, and registered direct mean-difference localizations in the corresponding high-signature contexts to determine whether the apparent amplification is supported as a localized effect. H153, SMARCA4-SMARCA2 modified by high signature C, is near-null (+0.0072; interval -0.0355 to +0.0500) and closed unresolved.  \n\nOverall, no new accepted claim is justified by this batch. The major accepted dataset associations remain unchanged: KEAP1 loss with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, TP53 loss with much weaker USP7 dependency, SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency, and CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 dependency in several accepted contexts. The new results mostly argue against MTAP-independent MDM2 effects and against major direct effects of constructed research markers/signatures, while leaving two signature-high interaction localizations as unresolved. Multiplicity is substantial, validation budget is exhausted, and all conclusions remain associations in this supplied lung-lineage dataset rather than causal or generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 12 appraise update. No voluntary validation budget remains, so no validation request. Direct MTAP tests after removing CDKN2A loss are negative/imprecise: H142 rejected; H143 unresolved/closed due sparse low-copy 9p21 cells. This further supports treating the robust CDKN2A-MDM2 association as a CDKN2A-locus/genomic-context marker, not evidence for MTAP-specific dependency. Direct marker tests are largely negative or not robustly clinically meaningful: H144 rejected, H145 rejected despite a statistically significant opposite USP7 association with marker E, H146 unresolved/closed, H147 rejected, H148 rejected. Interaction tests are mostly inconclusive: H149, H150, H153 unresolved/closed. H151 and H152 are the only promising new localizations because high signature A for CDKN2A-MDM2 and high signature B for KEAP1-NFE2L2 have point estimates above practical thresholds but wide intervals crossing null. These are left active, and direct mean-difference localizations were registered: CDKN2A loss among high signature A for MDM2, and KEAP1 loss among high signature B for NFE2L2. If those direct high-signature effects clear null and practical thresholds, consider accepted dataset-specific localization; if not, close the interaction claims as unsupported. Accepted major findings remain unchanged: KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency; TP53 loss -> weaker USP7 dependency; SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency; CDKN2A loss -> stronger MDM2 dependency broadly and in accepted localizations. All are in-dataset associations, not causal or generalizable claims."
